<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred-Amédée Dodds</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1842">1842</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred-Amédée Dodds was a French general of mixed African and European descent, born at Saint-Louis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>. He commanded the French expedition of 1892 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-franco-dahomean-war">Second Franco-Dahomean War</a>, defeating the army of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behanzin">Béhanzin</a> and taking the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a>; Béhanzin surrendered in 1894 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> became a French colony. Dodds later commanded French forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-amedee-dodds.jpg" alt="Alfred-Amédée Dodds" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Ogereau (1868-1908) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Dodds_in_1902.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dodds was born on 6 February 1842 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis, Senegal</a>, the eldest of 10 children. His father, Antoine Henri Dodds, was a merchant and director of the Saint-Louis post office, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> descent; his mother, Charlotte de la Chapelle, was a signare of French and African descent. His paternal grandfather, John Dodds, had been a British Army officer and aide-de-camp to the last English governor of Saint-Louis, and had married a Senegalese woman of French and African descent. He graduated from the École spéciale militaire de Saint-Cyr in 1862. At the beginning of the 20th century he was held up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-diaspora">African diaspora</a> as an example of African leadership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Dodds became a lieutenant in the Troupes de marine in 1867 and was posted to Réunion, where he distinguished himself during the riots of 1868. Made captain in December 1869, he fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870 and was made a Knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour. Captured, he escaped after the capitulation at Sedan, rejoined the Armée de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> and then the Armée de l'Est, and was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> at the end of the war.</p><p>He served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> from 1871 to 1878 and in Cochinchina from 1878 to 1879, then fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casamance">Casamance</a> from 1879 to 1883 as a battalion leader. As a lieutenant colonel he took part in the war of conquest in Tonkin, and as a colonel from 1887 in the counter-insurgency in the Fouta Djalon in French Guinea. He commanded French forces in Senegal from 1890 and in 1891 was given command of the Eighth Colonial Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>.</p><p>Close to the French Radical Party, he owed his appointment as superior commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> in 1892 to the personal intervention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a>. He was made général de brigade and Grand Officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour that year and General of Division in 1898. In 1900 he received the high command of colonial troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>, and from 1903 to 1907 he was high commander of the naval infantry and a member of the High Council of War. He died on 17 July 1922.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfred-Amédée Dodds” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1892 France sent a column of some two thousand men under Colonel <b>Alfred-Amédée Dodds</b>, which defeated the Dahomean army and entered Abomey in November 1892 as Behanzin withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After wars with the Kingdom of Dahomey in 1890 and 1892, General <b>Alfred-Amedee Dodds</b> took Abomey, and King Behanzin surrendered in 1894.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-dahomey">French Dahomey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.03,-16.5&amp;year=1894" aria-label="Saint-Louis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alfred-Amédée Dodds"><image href="/maps/globes/g330n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6099 6118h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6099 6118h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.03,-16.5&amp;year=1894">What was here in 1894? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
